{"reference":"Song of Songs 5","book":"Song of Songs","chapter":5,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Voices and Images","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The search scene includes a stark interruption: the city watchmen strike, wound, and take the searching woman’s wrap. The poem reports this violence; it does not command or approve it. The praise that follows uses imaginative comparisons—gold, doves, spices, ivory, sapphires, marble, and Lebanon—to describe the beloved’s body and presence. Such body-description poetry makes vivid gaze and comparison central to its expression of desire.","verse_refs":["Song of Songs 5:10-16","Song of Songs 5:2-8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_song_of_songs_005_e4c7b8e6eff5475907581f73"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The exchange with the daughters of Jerusalem helps distinguish voices in this chapter: one speaker appeals to them in longing, and they answer with a question about her beloved. Yet individual speaker assignments elsewhere in the Song are not always certain, so the dialogue should be read with that caution.","verse_refs":["Song of Songs 5:8-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_song_of_songs_005_6d10278c7725313f8ddbc9bc"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Song’s Wider Poetic World","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Across the Song, ancient Egyptian and Mesopotamian love poetry provides substantial analogies for its alternating voices, fragrance, landscapes, and praise of the beloved’s body. These parallels do not establish a single direct source or a cult ritual; they help situate the Song’s imagery within a wider world of love poetry.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_song_of_songs_005_748ff136224b7f2c0b4da735"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The Song’s recurring searches, descriptions, images, and refrains create poetic connections across the book, but they do not settle one uncontested continuous plot, wedding cycle, dream sequence, or biography. Major Jewish and Christian allegorical readings belong to the book’s later reception; the speakers themselves do not explicitly identify themselves with those theological figures.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_song_of_songs_005_efe68fb98e122294ebab99ab"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}